m1oVGc3dUU0emRyM1lWYVl5M19xVWZRQ1B3?oc=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": -7.08, "prior_sd": 5.11, "poll_margin": -0.5, "poll_sd": 11.79, "poll_weight": 0.16, "fundamentals": {"margin": -0.6, "lean": -6.0, "environment": 8.3, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": -0.9}, "rating_margin": -11.4, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.25, "baseline_margin": -6.04, "analyst_adjustment": -1.46, "key_factors": ["R+6 district lean with incumbent Flood's incumbency advantage", "Expert consensus of Likely-to-Solid R across ten raters", "September Democratic-sponsored poll showing a tied race, but thin and partisan-sponsored", "Flood's large fundraising edge ($2.4M vs $0.6M)"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Flood win in an R+6 district, and most raters call it Likely or Solid R, but a recent Democratic-sponsored poll showed a one-point race, suggesting Backemeyer is running closer than the district lean implies. A margin near R+7 balances the structural advantage against that late polling movement.", "watch": "Whether independent or GOP-sponsored polling confirms the tightening seen in the September Democratic poll, or whether Flood reasserts a clear lead after the debate cycle.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Mike Flood (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Republican Mike Flood seeks a third full term in Nebraska's 1st District against Democrat Chris Backemeyer, with a Libertarian and an independent also on the ballot. The district leans Republican by about six points, and Flood holds a large financial advantage, having raised roughly four times his challenger's total. Expert raters overwhelmingly place the race in the Likely or Solid Republican column, though a couple see it as more competitive.\n\nThe race has drawn attention through a series of late-September debates focused on Iran, tariffs, and rising prices, with both campaigns claiming the better performance. A Democratic-sponsored poll in early September showed the race essentially tied, a notable contrast to the district's lean, though the polling record is thin and one-sided in sponsorship. Prediction markets still price Flood as a strong favorite, and the balance of evidence points to a Republican hold with some genuine uncertainty about the final margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1466, "margin": -6.26}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1237, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1206, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2177, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1689, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1196, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1777, "margin": -6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2315, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1394, "margin": -7.5}]}